  "caseStudy": {
    "title": "The Door That Swings East",
    "background": "(Hypothetical case.) A family is considering a ground-floor independent house. A learner visits with the broker and notes: *\"This is an east-facing house; the main door opens inward towards the east.\"* The broker nods and the listing is updated to \"east-facing\". Later, the family's own consultant asks the learner how this was established.\n\nThe learner has a hand-drawn plot sketch (no north arrow) and a phone compass reading taken **inside the porch, next to the steel security gate**, which showed the door-swing direction roughly east. The road runs along the side of the house with the entrance facing the road.",
    "documentsReceived": "hand sketch of the plot (no north arrow, no date); one phone compass reading from the porch; the broker's verbal statement.",
    "documentsMissing": "approved site plan with north arrow; a second compass reading from an open area away from metal; a map/satellite check of the road's alignment; documented entrance position relative to the building outline; date and method of verification.",
    "wrongFirstReaction": "\"The door opens toward the east, so the house is east-facing.\" *This confuses door swing with entrance orientation, and relies on one compass reading taken next to a steel gate.*",
    "questions": [
      "What exactly did the learner observe, and what did they infer?",
      "Does the direction the door swings establish the entrance-facing direction? Why or why not?",
      "What problem exists with the compass reading, and how could it be improved?",
      "What does the learner need to establish before using a label such as \"east-facing\" in the report?",
      "Write one F / I / R entry that is honest about the uncertainty."
    ],
    "modelAnswer": [
      "**Observed:** a door that opens inward; one compass reading in the porch; a road along the side of the house. **Inferred (not observed):** that the house faces east.",
      "No. The swing is a hinge and design decision (for example to clear furniture or a passage). The entrance-facing direction comes from **the building outline and approach geometry**: which side of the building the entrance is on and which way a person faces when stepping out through it. A door on a north wall can swing towards the east, and the entrance still faces north.",
      "The reading was taken beside a steel gate, so it could be deflected. Take repeat readings in an open area away from metal; compare with a map or satellite view and, ideally, an approved plan with a north arrow; remember that magnetic and true north differ.",
      "(a) A verified north reference with its source; (b) the side of the building on which the entrance sits; (c) the approach direction; (d) date and method of verification.",
      "**F:** \"Main door is on the road-side wall and swings inward to the learner's right; one compass reading near a steel gate suggested east; no north arrow on sketch.\" **I:** \"Entrance-facing direction cannot be stated yet; door swing does not determine it and the compass reading may be disturbed.\" **R:** \"Obtain the approved site plan with north arrow, take repeat compass readings in the open and cross-check on a map; document the entrance side from the building outline. Ask the broker to hold the 'east-facing' label until verified.\""
    ],
    "lesson": "*The way a door opens tells you about the door. The direction an entrance faces tells you about the building. Record both separately, and do not label until the source is verified.*\n\n---"
  },
