    "title": "Two Doors, One Crowded Passage",
    "background": "A joint family of five (two elderly parents, a couple and a school-going child) lives in a third-floor flat in a small apartment society. The flat has a **corridor door** with a decorative nameplate, a **service door** in the kitchen opening onto a shared service balcony, and a **balcony door** from the living room. The family says: \"We always use the kitchen door when we come back from the market, because the lift is close to it and it is nearer to the kitchen. The front door is used by guests.\" The living-dining space is the main passage between the corridor door, the bedrooms and the bathroom. A dining table, a puja cabinet and a tall shoe rack are placed along this passage. The family asks: *\"Is our entrance direction wrong? And can the passage be made easier without breaking walls?\"* The only plan available is a society brochure plan, with no north arrow and no dimensions.",
    "documentsReceived": "society brochure plan (no north arrow, no dimensions); five photos of the living area; a hand-drawn sketch by the family showing furniture; a short note from the owners.",
