    "modelAnswer": [
      "Ask about the real pattern of use. The family uses the kitchen service door daily and the corridor door mainly for guests. Two reasonable approaches exist: treat the corridor door as the principal entrance because it is the flat's formal connection to the common corridor and lobby (the choice rests on that role and on guest use, not on the nameplate), and record the kitchen door as a secondary door used daily; or treat the kitchen door as principal because it carries the household's daily access, and explain why. Whichever is chosen, say which and why, and record both doors with their use. The balcony door is a secondary door. Note also that the service door opens to a shared service balcony, which may be subject to society rules.",
      "Very little. The plan has no north arrow, so **no direction can be stated as established**. Record \"entrance side as drawn; north not verified\" and arrange a proper north check. Pada: \"Pada not assessed: method and measurements not available.\" Do not call any direction wrong.",
      "Passage narrowed by the dining table, puja cabinet and shoe rack (observed in photos; width not measured); family reports elders bump into the table at night (reported); a through-movement from the service door via the kitchen into the living area may create crowding at meal times (inferred from the sketch). The family's own sketch is reported evidence, not verified measurement.",
      "Priority is **function and safety**: a clear, well-lit route for the elderly parents, and nothing blocking the corridor or any exit. If the shoe rack or any item sits in the common corridor, it may be a society or fire-safety matter. The society committee and, for any structural question, a qualified structural engineer or architect should be consulted. A traditional entrance reading comes only after north is verified, and stays secondary.",
      "**F:** \"The household uses the kitchen service door daily and the corridor door for guests (reported). The living-dining area is the main passage; a dining table, puja cabinet and tall shoe rack narrow it (photos, width not measured). Elders reportedly bump into the table at night. The plan has no north arrow.\" **I:** \"The corridor door is recorded as the principal entrance and the service door as a daily-use secondary door, with the reasoning stated. The passage is a functional constraint and a night-time safety concern for the elderly. No direction reading is made until north is verified; depending on the framework applied, one may be added later.\" **R (priority: Functional / Safety):** \"Measure the passage; move or replace the dining table with a smaller or round one; shift the shoe rack to the wall; add night lighting along the route; keep the corridor clear. Verify north on site and record the source. No wall changes are suggested; any structural question goes to a qualified professional.\""
    ],
    "lesson": "*The principal entrance is a matter of recorded use and reasoning, not decoration; without a verified plan, say what you cannot say; and fix the passage with furniture before thinking about walls.*"
