      "**Facts:** kitchen is in the north-east portion (orientation verified from the builder's north arrow); it shares a wet wall with the utility area; a plumbing shaft is adjacent; one window opens to an external shaft; the south-east corner is a bedroom.",
      "The classical general framework commonly teaches the **south-east** for kitchens, with **north-west** as a frequently cited alternative. Schools differ, and this is an interpretive preference, not an engineering fact.",
      "Ventilation and exhaust, stove clearances, gas safety, fire-escape access, daylight, cleanliness, water drainage, work-flow and whether the cook is comfortable.",
      "Improve ventilation and exhaust (professionally fitted); reorganise appliances and storage for a safe work triangle; improve lighting; keep the stove away from the window draught and from combustible fabrics; keep the kitchen door area clear. Consider traditional-minded household preferences (such as the cook's facing, discussed on Day 6) only where they do not conflict with safety.",
      "**F:** \"Kitchen lies in the north-east of the verified plan; it shares a plumbing shaft; window opens to a shaft; no exhaust specification supplied.\" **I:** \"Under the classical general framework the south-east is commonly taught for kitchens; this kitchen's position differs. Schools vary, and the position is fixed by services.\" **R:** \"Document as a deviation. Priority 1 (safety/functional): have a qualified professional check exhaust, ventilation and gas arrangements. Priority 2 (functional): improve layout and lighting. Priority 3 (traditional): note preferences without costly change. Do not relocate unless an engineer and plumber confirm feasibility and the household still wants it.\""
