  "caseStudyB": {
    "title": "Real-World Case Study B — \"The Staircase in the Middle\" *(self-study / optional extension)*",
    "background": "*(Hypothetical case. Framework applied: classical general framework, as in the Framework Reference table above.)* The Kulkarni family owns a two-storey independent house in a town near Pune, built about twenty-five years ago by a local contractor. A well-meaning relative visited and said, \"The staircase is right in the middle of the house, in the Brahmasthan. That is a big fault. Remove it and build a new one outside.\" The staircase is an internal, concrete, dog-leg staircase that also supports the first-floor landing. The ground floor has a toilet tucked beside the staircase, a small pooja shelf in the north-east corner of the living room, and the elders' bedroom at the back. The family asks: *\"Do we need to demolish the staircase?\"*",
    "documentsReceived": "a hand-drawn plan from the contractor (no north arrow, not to scale); mobile photos of each room; a short list of the family's daily routines (elders use the ground-floor bedroom; two teenagers use the first floor).",
    "documentsMissing": "a verified north (compass reading on site), structural drawings, the municipal sanction plan, the plumbing layout for the toilet beside the stairs, the electrical panel location, any record of alterations since construction.",
    "wrongFirstReaction": "\"The staircase is in the centre, so it is a defect. Demolish it and rebuild it outside, and move the toilet too.\" *This treats a framework preference as a defect, ignores that a staircase may be structural and a safety route, skips orientation, and recommends demolition with no engineer's opinion.*",
    "questions": [
      "What must be verified before any directional or centre-of-plot comment?",
      "What does the framework table say about the centre of the plot, and how firmly can you state it?",
      "What practical and safety questions come before any change to the staircase?",
      "List two or three low-disruption, non-structural options for the stair area and the adjacent toilet.",
      "Write one F / I / R entry that is honest about limits."
    ],
    "modelAnswer": [
      "**Orientation and the drawing itself:** take a compass reading on site (two readings, away from metal), record it as the source of north, and measure two or three distances to check the hand-drawn plan against the real rooms. Until then, any directional comment is provisional. Also check what has been altered since construction.",
      "The table says the plot centre (Brahmasthan) is commonly taught to be **kept open and uncluttered, avoiding heavy loads, toilets and staircases**. It is a plot-level preference; schools differ in how strictly they apply it, and it is an interpretive preference, not an engineering fact. Write: \"differs from the preferred pattern\", not \"fault\".",
      "Is the staircase structural or does it carry load? Is it the only safe exit route? Condition of treads, handrails, headroom, lighting, anti-slip finish, landing width; whether the toilet beside the stairs leaks or has proper ventilation; electrical wiring in the stair area; whether elders can use it safely. These are safety and maintenance points and come first.",
      "Keep the stair area clear of storage and shoes; improve lighting and add a handrail or anti-slip strips (using a qualified contractor where fitting is needed); keep the space under or around the stair uncluttered if the household wishes to respect the framework's openness idea; check and repair any leak near the toilet and improve its ventilation; keep the toilet door closed with a simple door closer. Moving or removing the staircase is a structural matter, to be considered, if at all, only with a structural engineer and municipal rules.",
      "**F:** \"The internal staircase lies near the centre of the ground floor on an unverified plan; it supports the first-floor landing; a toilet lies beside it; the pooja shelf lies in the north-east corner of the living room; north has not yet been verified; no structural or plumbing drawings were supplied.\" **I:** \"Under the classical general framework the plot centre is commonly taught to be kept open of staircases and toilets; this house differs. Schools vary, and a staircase of this kind may be structural.\" **R:** \"Document as a deviation, provisional until north and the plan are verified. Priority 1 (safety/maintenance): condition of stairs, handrail, lighting, toilet leak and ventilation. Priority 2 (functional): clear the stair area; improve circulation. Priority 3 (traditional): note the framework's openness idea; no demolition advised. Any structural change only after an engineer's review and if the household still wishes it. Unknown: structural role of the stair, plumbing layout, municipal approvals. No outcome is promised.\""
    ],
    "lesson": "*A framework idea about the centre of the plot is documented honestly, but a staircase is first a safety route and possibly a structural element. Verify, check, then talk about change.*"
  },
