    "caseStudyB":  {
                       "title":  "The Wrong Zone, the Real Problems",
                       "frameworkUsed":  "*(Hypothetical case. Framework used: classical general framework as set out in the Framework Reference Sheet.)*",
                       "background":  "A young couple has bought a resale flat on the second floor of an older building. The builder\u0027s brochure plan, which carries a north arrow of unknown source, places the kitchen in the **north-east portion**. A visiting acquaintance tells them, \"This kitchen is in the wrong place; you must break the wall and shift it, and paint it yellow to correct it.\" The couple are worried and ask you for a view. On the visit you see: a small kitchen with one window that has been sealed with a steel grille and a stack of cartons; an old chimney whose duct ends inside the false ceiling; a damp, discoloured patch on the cabinet base under the sink; a refrigerator wedged into a niche with no gap at the back; and a rubber gas hose that runs behind the hob close to the hot side panel. The cylinder stands in the open corner next to the doorway.",
                       "documentsReceived":  "the brochure plan (north arrow source unknown), three phone photographs of the kitchen, the couple\u0027s description of the acquaintance\u0027s advice.",
                       "documentsMissing":  "a site-verified north direction; building drawing showing plumbing shafts and structure; chimney and hob manuals; gas installation and hose details (age, date of replacement); electrical layout and earthing information; society rules on alterations.",
                       "wrongFirstReaction":  "\"The kitchen is in the north-east, which is a bad zone. Agree that it must be shifted, apply the yellow paint, and tell them things will improve once it is corrected.\" *This calls a departure a defect, recommends a costly structural change without any checks, treats colour as a remedy, promises an outcome, and ignores the real safety and ventilation issues.*",
                       "questions":  [
                                         "Which statements in the case are observable facts, and which are interpretations?",
                                         "Is the north-east position a defect? What is the correct way to record it, and what must be verified first?",
                                         "Which findings are practical or safety priorities, in order?",
                                         "Who should be asked to check each priority, and what should the learner not do?",
                                         "Write one O-I-R entry for the kitchen position and one for the ventilation/gas concern."
                                     ],
                       "modelAnswer":  [
                                           "Facts: kitchen in the north-east portion of the brochure plan; window sealed with a grille and cartons; chimney duct ends in the false ceiling; damp patch under the sink; refrigerator without a rear gap; gas hose behind the hob near a hot panel; cylinder stands in the open near the doorway. Interpretations: \"the kitchen is in the wrong place\"; \"break the wall and shift it\"; \"yellow paint will correct it\".",
                                           "No. A kitchen away from the commonly taught south-east (with the north-west named as a common alternative) is a **departure from a preferred traditional position**, to be documented as an interpretive point. The north arrow\u0027s source is unknown, so even the position needs to be verified. This course does not label zones as bad or promise a result from changes.",
                                           "Priority order: (a) gas hose near a hot panel and the cylinder position, a safety concern for a licensed gas fitter; (b) ventilation, as the window is blocked and the duct ends in the false ceiling; (c) the damp patch and possible leak under the sink; (d) the refrigerator\u0027s lack of airflow; (e) the position of the kitchen, documented as a departure; (f) colour, optional and aesthetic only.",
                                           "A licensed gas fitter for the hose, regulator and cylinder; a ventilation/chimney installer for the duct; a plumber for the leak and a licensed electrician for the sockets and the refrigerator connection; a structural professional or the society before any wall is touched. The learner should not move gas parts, open the false ceiling, or advise the couple to break the wall.",
                                           "**Position - F:** \"Kitchen is in the north-east portion of the brochure plan; north arrow source unknown; not verified on site.\" **I:** \"The south-east is commonly taught as the preferred kitchen zone and the north-west as a common alternative; schools differ. A different position is recorded as a departure, not a defect, and only after the direction is verified.\" **R:** \"Verify north using a reliable method. Document the departure. Do not recommend relocation: it involves structure, plumbing, gas, electrical and ventilation. Consider low-disruption improvements first. Colour is optional and aesthetic only. No outcome is promised.\"\n**Ventilation/gas - F:** \"Window blocked by a grille and cartons; chimney duct ends inside the false ceiling; rubber gas hose passes near the hot side panel; cylinder is in an open corner near the doorway.\" **I:** \"Smoke and heat may not be leaving the room and the hose may be exposed to heat; both are practical safety concerns that apply whatever the direction.\" **R:** \"Priority: safety. Do not use the hob until the hose has been inspected by a licensed gas fitter; clear the window and cartons; ask a ventilation installer to check the duct route; have a plumber inspect under the sink; leave a gap at the back of the refrigerator as the manufacturer\u0027s instructions require. No outcome is promised.\""
                                       ],
                       "lesson":  "*A departure from tradition is a note, not an alarm. The real work is the hose, the air and the damp.*"
                   },
