                                          "No. Available space and household needs vary, and prayer practices are not identical in every household. A **stable, clean niche or cabinet** can serve well. Reassure the family that the home is not \"unsuitable\"; the preference is a traditional guideline, not a rule that every home must meet.",
                                          "Stable base for the lamp; non-combustible surface; clearance from fabrics and furniture; never unattended; ventilation (open window or airflow); electrical sockets used safely; the elderly worshipper\u0027s ease of access and a clear exit path.",
                                          "Document it. State the commonly taught preference (North-East, with East or North as variations), note the corner is not available, and recommend the nearest feasible alternative on the **east or north side of the living area** if the family wishes, **after** discussing their preference. No construction.",
                                          "**F:** \"Plan shows no separate prayer room; east wall of the living room is free, about 1.2 m wide; a curtain hangs 0.4 m from the proposed shelf.\" **I:** \"Many sources describe the North-East, East or North as commonly taught preferences for a prayer space. Schools and family customs vary. A dedicated room is not mandatory.\" **R:** \"Discuss the family\u0027s preference. If agreed, place a firmly fixed or freestanding shelf on the east wall with a non-combustible tray for the lamp, move the curtain away or shorten it, ensure ventilation, and check the socket. No structural change. Do not move the family\u0027s sacred objects without consent.\""
