    "id":  "day-11",
    "day":  11,
    "lang":  "en",
    "title":  "Storage, Safe, Staircase, Granary and Auxiliary Spaces",
    "edition":  "Astrosutras by Anoopam · English edition · PILOT DRAFT",
    "meta":  {
                 "minutes":  90,
                 "teachMinutes":  60,
                 "practicalMinutes":  30,
                 "level":  "Beginner",
                 "frameworkNote":  "Vastu is taught here as a traditional interpretive framework. Nothing in this course guarantees health, wealth, relationship or career outcomes. A safe\u0027s position, a pantry\u0027s direction or a staircase\u0027s location is never a substitute for real security, fire safety, structural soundness or sound housekeeping.",
                 "timeNote":  "**Self-study / optional extension (outside the 90 minutes):** the \"Go deeper\" sub-sections, Real-World Case Study B, the Mini-cases and the Extra Practice are self-study material. Allow roughly 60-90 extra minutes if you do them all, spread across the day or the weekend. The guided 90-minute session works completely without them."
             },
    "learningQuestion":  "The principal rooms are done. What about the cupboards, the safe, the stairs, the pantry and the utility rooms, and how do I assess them without letting tradition override safety?",
    "objectives":  [
                       "Assess storage and pantry areas for organisation, access and environmental conditions (moisture, pests, ventilation).",
                       "Record **traditional interpretations of safe placement** separately from **real security needs**.",
                       "Review a staircase for visible safety features (headroom, handrails, lighting, tread consistency, slip resistance, landings).",
                       "Include **auxiliary spaces** (utility, laundry, garage, staff quarters, terrace) in a whole-property assessment.",
                       "Flag items that need **specialist review** (structural, electrical, fire, security) instead of deciding them yourself."
                   ],
